## 24. Three Visible Faces and Hidden Faces

Suppose:

```text
A ↔ D
B ↔ E
C ↔ F
```

If a corner contains:

```text
A, B, C
```

the opposite corner contains:

```text
D, E, F
```

Diagram:

```text
Corner 1                  Opposite corner

   A                            D
  / \                          / \
 B---C                        E---F
```

---

## 25. Corner Compatibility

Every valid corner contains exactly one member from each opposite pair.

Suppose opposite pairs are:

```text
1 ↔ 6
2 ↔ 5
3 ↔ 4
```

A corner cannot contain:

```text
1, 6, 2
```

because `1` and `6` are opposites.

A valid corner could be:

```text
1, 2, 3
1, 2, 4
1, 5, 3
6, 2, 4
```

and so on, depending on the actual handedness of the die.

#### Extremely fast test

> Three faces at one corner must never contain an opposite pair.

---

## 26. Counting Possible Corners

If the opposite pairs are:

```text
(A,a)
(B,b)
(C,c)
```

then each corner chooses one face from each pair.

Therefore there are:

```text
2 × 2 × 2 = 8
```

possible face combinations if we ignore orientation.

These correspond to the 8 vertices of the cube.

---

## 27. Eight Vertex Combinations

For pairs:

```text
1 ↔ 6
2 ↔ 5
3 ↔ 4
```

the possible vertex sets are:

```text
1,2,3
1,2,4
1,5,3
1,5,4

6,2,3
6,2,4
6,5,3
6,5,4
```

These are the eight possible three-face combinations, assuming the opposite pairs are fixed.

---

## 28. Important Subtlety

The 8 combinations above describe which **sets** can meet at corners.

They do not by themselves describe every possible spatial orientation.

For rotation questions, the order/orientation of the faces matters.

So:

```text
SET CHECK
```

and

```text
ORIENTATION CHECK
```

are two different tests.

---

## 29. Dice Net

A **net** is a flat arrangement of six squares that can be folded into a cube.

One common net:

```text
          +---+
          | 2 |
          +---+
+---+---+---+---+
| 5 | 1 | 3 | 6 |
+---+---+---+---+
          | 4 |
          +---+
```

When folded:

```text
1 = front
3 = right
5 = left
6 = back
2 = top
4 = bottom
```

Therefore:

```text
1 ↔ 6
2 ↔ 4
3 ↔ 5
```

---

## 30. Net Opposite Rule

For many cube nets:

- Squares directly attached to the central face become adjacent.
- The square that folds around to the opposite side becomes the opposite face.

A useful way:

```text
       TOP
        |
LEFT -- CENTER -- RIGHT
        |
      BOTTOM

BACK = square attached in the appropriate outer position
```

The exact opposite relation depends on the net geometry.

---

## 31. Folding a Net — Mental Method

Imagine the center square is fixed.

Then mentally fold each neighbor upward:

```text
Flat:

      T
      |
L --- C --- R
      |
      B
```

After folding:

```text
         T
         |
     L --C-- R
         |
         B

       BACK behind C
```

The important concept:

> Four squares around a center become the four side faces around that center.

---

## 32. Invalid Dice Net

Not every arrangement of six squares can form a cube.

Example of a suspicious arrangement:

```text
+---+---+---+---+---+---+
| 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
+---+---+---+---+---+---+
```

A straight line of six squares cannot directly fold into a cube.

For cube reasoning:

> Six faces must be arranged so that, after folding, exactly six distinct cube faces are formed without overlap.

---
